bc for Sybian REAL -- bc-compatible subset (E32 port)
=====================================================

SOURCE PROVENANCE (honest disclosure)
-------------------------------------
GNU bc 1.07 was downloaded from https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/bc/bc-1.07.tar.gz as
a reference.  It is ~14k lines of C plus a flex scanner and a Bison grammar,
and depends on stdio, malloc, getopt and libm.  That cannot be ported verbatim
to a freestanding, no-libc E32 process with a 64 KiB heap budget, so this
directory contains a FAITHFUL, FROM-SCRATCH bc-compatible SUBSET, not GNU bc
source code.  The arithmetic semantics (decimal digit arrays, scale rules,
truncating division/remainder, right-associative power) follow GNU bc 1.07.

Files
-----
  bignum.h / bignum.c   base-10 arbitrary-precision engine (no libc, bump arena)
  bcinterp.h / bcinterp.c  recursive-descent parser + tree-walk interpreter
  README                this file

Language subset implemented
---------------------------
  * arbitrary-precision decimal numbers (integer + fractional scale)
  * operators: + - * / % ^ (right-assoc), unary -, comparisons
    (< <= > >= == !=), logical !, assignment = and += -= *= /= %= ^=
  * `scale` variable controls division precision (GNU bc rule)
  * variables a-z plus `scale`
  * define ... { ... } functions, parameters, return, recursion, if/else,
    while, for, break, continue, { } blocks, print (numbers and strings)
  * comments: # to end of line, // to end of line, /* ... */
  * statements separated by `;` or newline; multiple per input line

Limitations vs real bc
----------------------
  * no `auto` locals (parameters are saved/restored per call; other names are
    globals), no arrays, no `length`/`sqrt`/math library functions, no `-l`
  * single-letter variable names only (function names may be longer)
  * digits per value capped at 160; expression arena 48 KiB; max 200k loop
    iterations and 16 nested calls (deliberate guards for a freestanding host)
  * print emits one BC: marker per statement rather than raw stdout bytes

Memory
------
The interpreter uses a 48 KiB bump arena reset per top-level statement plus
fixed variable/function tables.  Total static footprint is under 64 KiB,
matching the process heap budget.  No hosted libc calls are made.
